    "body": "I'm afraid not. I've written a tool that would (currently I'm using the custom-element solution and it processes those), but it is not publicly available, let alone widely deployed.\n\nAn advantage of a standardized `for` attribute, would be that the `abbr` element doesn't have to immediately follow the `dfn` element. For example given:\n\n```html\n<dfn id=\"dfn-html\">HyperText Markup Language</dfn>\n...\n...\n...\n<abbr for=\"dfn-html\">HTML</abbr>\n```\n\nBrowsers could leverage this `for` attribute to provide a better UX (as they do today with the same attribute on `label`), e.g., by rendering the `abbr` element (a) with a tooltip by taking the text contents of the `dfn` element (where the presence of a `title` attribute would override this behavior) and/or (b) as a link to the definition.",
    "author_association": "NONE",
    "pin": null,
